m is is something that if  this was written for the first time  now I think yeah okay but just knowing  how long ago he wrote this and like dang  I I don't know how well the book did  when it came out I can't imagine it was  a massive splash because I'd never heard  of it before this but I'm surprised it  doesn't come up more often in terms of  like major influences on the direction  that vampires in fiction have taken and  the only reason that I can think off  atop my head is that it didn't sell well  enough to be a major influence but  there's a lot of concepts that are  pretty standard now in terms of the  nature of vampires and the approach to  their characterization that was pretty  new for 1982 and that rhyme that I'm  really embarrassed by that but I was I  was quite impressed with this if I if I  was gonna make one particular criticism  and it is a very particular one as I  said this is set primarily about 80 85  percent of it timeline-wise  is in the pre Civil War South so there  is a language used that I will not  repeat on camera I think you know I'm  saying where I was okay reading it in  dialogue however it was a word that also  just showed up as a descriptor like in  narrative not that a character said it  but like the narration says it and that  like made me real uncomfortable like I  suppose the justification is the  narrative is always still through the  perspective of either Abner Marsh or  sour Billy Tipton and they especially  sour Billy actually would think in those  terms so I'm not gonna say there's no  narrative justification for  I will say that that that threw me off  as someone who was prepared to give a  pass to the use of the word in dialogue  given the time of the setting so that  threw me off a little bit I know that  Martin can get criticized for abuse of  female characters basically there wasn't  a ton of that there is some but again a  lot of it has to do with sort of is  within the context of kind of what you  expect out of a vampire story vampires  like young nubile prey so yeah that said  for how much given the premise how much  more of that there could have been not  nearly as much as I kind of was afraid  was gonna be in there so that that was  actually a mild relief but it is there  so if that kind of depictions sexual  violence if that's if that's an issue  for you again not it's not pervasive  across the book but it is in there so  you'd want to be aware of that the thing  is I haven't really talked about the  plot because the plot ultimately is  surprisingly straightforward in terms of  marsh hooks up with a rich guy that's  Joshua York who offers to finance him  building a brand new massive fast  steamboat and we eventually learn over  time more about Joshua and who he is and  what he's doing and then eventually they  come into conflict with Damon Julian and  it's not a massive twisty turny story  except it also kind of is because it  kept building to moments that I thought  I knew what was going to happen  then it pivoted ever so slightly for  things to go in a slightly different  direction for that not like a massive we  yank the rug out from under you but just  a little like oh no no not that way here  here that way yeah so it's just little  nudges in terms of the directions of the  story and and where it goes and the  other thing is for being a fairly  straightforward an ultimately relatively  small story just about a handful of  characters and ultimately about Abner  Marsh more than anybody else it feels  much bigger because of something you can  kind of get away with and a novel that  doesn't work as well and TV and I feel  really doesn't work on film which is  that even though the narrative is this  pretty restricted period of time you can  have a chapter or two that is just  Joshua York telling his own personal  history to Marsh and to us the audience  and suddenly we get this whole back end  of the story filled in and you know we  learn more about Damon Julianne and we  get 